<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mirabai</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1498–c. 1547</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Meera Bai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mirabai was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> princess and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> mystic poet of the bhakti movement, devoted to the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>. Born into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rathore">Rathore</a> family of Merta, she is traditionally said to have married Bhoj <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">Raj</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rana-sanga">Rana Sanga</a> of Mewar, and after his early death to have rejected the conventions of a royal widow to live as a devotee. Hundreds of devotional songs attributed to her are still sung across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mirabai.jpg" alt="Mirabai" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kangra_painting_of_Mirabai,_the_female_Bhakti_saint.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
